he scientific consensus on  voter fraud is clear as glass the  Brennan Center for Justice at  and why use law-school reviewed all  relevant literature on the subject and  put out a report called debunking the  voter fraud myth in January of this year  the report found that putting rhetoric  aside to look at the facts makes clear  that fraud by voters at the polls is  vanishingly rare and does not happen on  a scale even close to that necessary to  quote rigged an election a comprehensive  study published by a Columbia University  political science professor found that  number one voter fraud is extremely rare  number two a lack of evidence of voter  fraud is not because of a failure to  codify it and number three most voter  fraud allegations turn out to be  something other than fraud there's a  2016 paper from four Ivy League  professors and a Microsoft researcher  which concluded that the upper limit on  double voting in the 2012 election was  0.02 percent the upper limit the paper  noted that the incident rate was likely  much lower considering that the  researchers showed that quote many if  not all of these apparent double votes  could be a result of measurement error a  2016 analysis from Dartmouth found no  evidence of widespread voter fraud of  any kind a 2014 analysis from Harvard  found that quote the likely percent of  non citizen voters in recent US  elections is zero Richard L Hazen a law  professor at the University of  California Irvine and author of the  voting wars went back thirty years to  try to find a single example where quote  an election outcome could plausibly have  turned on voter impersonation fraud and  was unable to find one in one of the  most comprehensive investigations of  voter fraud to date Justin Levitt of  Loyola Law School was only able to  identify 31 credible allegations of  voter impersonation out of more than 1  billion ballots cast between 2000 and  2014  that's roughly point zero zero zero zero  zero zero zero three one percent of  votes which would be the result of  in-person voter fraud the figure may  actually be even lower according to  because he merely counted all credible  claims not prosecutions or convictions  voter impersonation is just one of  several tactics that can theoretically  be used to commit voter fraud Levitz  figure is an important one though  because the bulk of state laws aimed  preventing voter fraud are voter ID laws  which require registered voters to  provide government ID before casting a  ballot Levitt wrote in a 2014 op-ed in  the Washington Post that requirements to  show ID at the polls are designed for  pretty much one thing people showing up  at the polls pretending to be somebody  else in order to each cast one  incremental fake ballot this is a very  slow clunky and difficult way to steal  an election which is why it rarely  happens so essentially the voter ID law  is pushed by Republicans around the  country are totally ineffective in  stopping voter fraud and we'll get to  that in more detail later on Kansas  secretary of state Kris Kobach the man  who's now leading Donald Trump's voter  fraud crusade on a national level became  the only secretary of state in the  nation with the power to prosecute voter  fraud cases in 2015 after claiming that  quote there is a significant problem in  Kansas and in the rest of the country of  aliens getting on our voting rolls Kobuk  ultimately failed to prosecute a single  case of non-citizens committing voter  fraud Kobuk also examined 84 million  votes cast in 22 states to look for  cases of duplicate registration that  project yielded 14 prosecutions  representing point zero zero zero zero  one seven percent of the votes cast an  exhaustive Washington Post investigation  found only four documented cases of  voter fraud during the 2016 election  these included a trump supporter who  tried to vote twice in Iowa a trump  supporter who tried to vote twice in  Texas supposedly to quote test the  system and a woman who tried to cast a  ballot for a recently deceased spouse a  woman in Florida was also caught marking  empty bubbles on absentee ballots but  there's no evidence she changed  single presidential vote a 2017 analysis  from the Washington Post found that  there is quote no evidence to support  Trump's claims that people from  Massachusetts were bused in to New  Hampshire to vote against him and Kelly  Ayotte so where does all this hysteria  come from in the first place what if any  evidence do these conservatives have to  hold up in the face of the mountain of  evidence that seems to disprove their  claims earlier I showed you a clip of  White House spokesperson Stephen Miller  who told George Stephanopoulos that  quote 14% of non-citizens according to  academic research at a minimum are  registered to vote what academic  research was Stephen Miller referring to  turns out that Miller was referring to  research by Old Dominion University  professors that use data from the 2008  and 2010 elections now the Old Dominion  study didn't examine voter rolls or even  uncover any direct evidence of any  illegal immigrants voting but instead it  relied on voluntary polling data of  adult Hispanic Americans according to  the Old Dominion researchers 14 percent  of the survey several hundred  respondents who identified themselves as  non-citizens also said that they're  registered to vote the researchers then  extrapolated this finding to suggest  that there are potentially you know  millions of non-citizens lurking in the  shadows of our voter rolls casting  ballots that cancel out the franchise of  American citizens Stephen and Saleh  bahir a Harvard professor who manages  the data used in the Old Dominion study  said that the data was misused and the  study's conclusions are wrong and solid  bahir was adamant arguing that quote  their finding is entirely due to  measurement error adding that quote the  Old Dominion study is an incorrect use  of the survey that we manage and a false  claim of evidence of non citizen voting  it's a dangerous stray false fact and  solid bahir explained that quote we  asked people in successive years their  citizenship that minimizes the error  upon doing so we find  no instances of voting among people  stating consistently that they are  non-citizens even one of the Old  Dominion researchers Jesse Richmond said  to the Washington Post that quote there  has been a tendency to miss read our  results as proof of massive voter fraud  which we don't think they are so one of  the very researchers who Stephen Miller  cited says the data does not mean what  Miller claims it means a similar study  conducted by McLaughlin and Associates  in 2013 suggested that over 2 million  non-citizens may be registered to vote  in the US the study has been trotted out  by conservative publications like The  Washington Times and Infowars in a  desperate attempt to back up Trump and  Miller's claims about voter fraud but  the McLaughlin and Associates study  relies on the same flawed method as the  Old Dominion study quote cherry-picking  low frequency events in large sample  surveys as unsolvable here puts it and  as for Trump's outlandish claim that 3  to 5 million illegal aliens voted in the  2016 presidential election costing him  the popular vote can you guess where  that claim originated Infowars the  origin of trumps wacky claims can be  traced back to a Facebook post from Alex  Jones that reads quote 3 million votes  in the US presidential election were  cast by illegal aliens according to Greg  Phillips of the vote fraud dot-org  organization as it turns out however  there is no such report by vote fraud  org it doesn't exist Greg Phillips who  founded an organization called vote  stand said to PolitiFact that he's not  even affiliated with vote fraud org  rather the claims made by Infowars  correspond back to a series of since  deleted tweets from Phillips's account  from just after the November election on  November 11 Phillips tweeted completed  analysis of database of 180 million  voter registrations number of non  citizen votes exceeds 3 million  consulting legal team then on the 13th  of November he followed up with we have  verified more than 3 million votes cast  by non-citizens we are joining true the  votes to initiate legal action hashtag  unwritten little conservative that's  worked with the Alabama and Mississippi  Republican parties would not provide any  additional information to back up his  claims when confronted by PolitiFact  Phillips would also not say what the  data is or where it came from or what  methodology he used so we have no idea  how Phillips arrived at his conclusion  Phillips's only academic credentials are  a BS in business administration from the  University of Alabama  so Phillips is no expert in complex data  analytics and he clearly has an agenda  to try to show that voter fraud is a  major issue but nonetheless Phillips and  his claims have been taken seriously and  have been given a lot of attention by  some on the right Trump himself even  cited Phillips in January tweeting quote  look forward to seeing final results of  vote stand Greg Phillips and Cru say at  least three million votes were illegal  we must do better in response to  questions about the source of Crump's  three to five million illegal votes  claim then press secretary Sean Spicer  had this to say the President does  believe that he has stated that before I  think he stated his concerns voter fraud  and people voting illegally during the  campaign and he continues to maintain  that belief based on studies and  evidence that people have presented to  him Thank You Ryan today said there's no  evidence the National Association of  secretaries of state say that they don't  agree with the president's assessment  what evidence do you have as I said I  think the president has believed that  for a while based on studies information  he had presumably the supposed evidence  Spicer was referring to is Phillips's  November tweets and the corresponding  Infowars story as long as Phillips  refuses to release the data he's  referring to and defend its methodology  there is absolutely no reason to  consider his claims credible in all  likelihood Phillips probably used the  same flaw discredited method that the  Old Dominion professors used back in  2014 so again we're left with absolutely  no evidence that non-citizens vote in  meaningful numbers in a way that has any  impact on our elections the idea that  millions of illegal immigrants vote in  US elections makes no intuitive sense  either Wendy Weiser the director of the  democracy program at the Brennan Center  for Justice and Douglas Keith who's the  cat's fellow at the Brennan Center  explained in an op-ed for time that  quote it is relatively easy for a non  citizen to get caught if an ineligible  person intentionally registers or votes  not only do they risk being recognized  but their name will be on permanent  lists of voters for later detection and  as discussed below  lots of people scrutinized those lists  looking for potentially ineligible  voters they also add that quote even  more serious a non citizen can be  deported for casting a single vote they  were not eligible to cast for  non-citizens working towards  naturalization merely being registered  to vote can serve as a basis for denying  citizenship so again the idea that  millions or even thousands of  undocumented people in the US are  risking fines imprisonment and  deportation just to game the system and  cast one vote is preposterous but  Republicans play into the idea in an  effort to fearmonger to their base and  support not only their anti-immigration  agenda but also their politically  charged efforts to create tighter voting  restrictions and this is where the  really concerning aspect of this whole  issue comes into play the problem is  that the whole issue of voter fraud is  really a red herring it's a pretense  that Republicans have used for decades  to argue for voting restrictions such as  voter ID laws that ultimately serve to  disenfranchise significant portions of  vulnerable demographics that have been  shown to disproportionately vote for  Democrats the Brookings Institution  argued in a 2016 report that these voter  ID laws systematically disenfranchised  minorities young people and the poor  adding that they are only one of several  practices that add up to a widespread  effort at election discrimination  others include purges of the voting  rolls reductions in early voting  opportunities and the closing of polling  stations voter ID laws currently exist  in 20 states and the practices of  purging voter rolls in  early voting and the number of polling  stations is widespread throughout the  country in 2016 under the guise of  improving election integrity the  Republican dominated North Carolina  legislature enacted a voter ID law that  also eliminated same-day voter  registration and eliminated seven days  of early voting as well as putting an  end to out of precinct voting it was  struck down by a federal court which  found that the law had discriminatory  intent and that it targeted  african-americans with almost surgical  precision thousands of pages of  documents were released as part of the  case and some showed Republican  lawmakers specifically asking for data  sorted by race the judges noted that the  data that the North Carolina lawmakers  requested before crafting the law showed  that African Americans  disproportionately lacked the most  common kind of photo ID those issued by  the Department of Motor Vehicles the  data also showed that black voters were  more likely to make use of early voting  and that counties with Sunday voting in  2014 were disproportionately black and  Democratic after reviewing that data the  legislature passed the bill eliminating  early voting the judges wrote that thus  in what comes as close to a smoking gun  as we are likely to see in modern times  the states very justification for a  challenged statute hinges explicitly on  race specifically it's concerned that  African Americans who had overwhelmingly  voted for Democrats had too much access  to the franchise while governor pat  mccrory insisted that the North Carolina  laws only purpose was to combat voter  fraud longtime Republican consultant  Carter Wren who the Washington Post  described as a fixture in North Carolina  politics admitted that of course it's  political why else would you do it  look if African Americans voted  overwhelmingly Republican they would  have kept early voting right where it  was  Renne said it wasn't about  discriminating against African Americans  they just ended up in the middle of it  because they vote for Democrats these  racist voter restriction laws are a  completely insidious cowardly pathetic  shameful attempt by Republicans to  suppress the vote of vulnerable  demographics who won't vote for them  instead of going out and trying to  earn people's votes Republicans are  trying to shut them out of the ballot  box and robbed them of their right to  vote in the context of our country's  long and ugly history of barring people  of color from the ballot box the  discriminatory aspect of these voter  suppression laws is outright shameful  but even still the targeting of certain  demographics has not been exclusively  reserved for minorities students and  poor whites have also become the targets  of the Republican crusade to suppress  the vote in leaked emails Green Bay City  Clerk  Chris teske an appointee of Republican  mayor Jim Schmidt admitted that the city  was trying to hinder college students  from voting because they tend to vote  Democratic leaders of eight different  student groups at the University of  wisconsin-green Bay had asked the city  to put an early voting location on  campus to alleviate long lines which  prevented many students from voting in  the 2016 primaries the request was  denied and in an email to a colleague on  the matter Teske wrote I feel by asking  for this to be the site for early voting  is encouraging the students to vote more  than benefiting the city as a whole I  have heard it said that students lean  more toward the Democrats a lawsuit  filed in Alabama in 2015 cites the  example of a woman from a rural area who  can't vote because she lacks a driver's  license according to the lawsuit she  needs to get a state issued voter ID at  the DMV but the one nearest her is open  one day per month there's no public  transportation to another DMV 40 miles  away roundtrip with more opening hours  according to a study from NYU's Brennan  Center 11% of voting-age citizens  nationally lack necessary photo ID while  many people in rural areas have trouble  accessing ID offices just think of the  burden that these restrictions put on  people who lack the time and resources  necessary to acquire proper ID or the  countless people who were discouraged  from voting because of needlessly long  lines caused by local polling stations  being closed while voter fraud is a  phantom issue intentional voter  suppression at the hands of Republican  state officials is a very real danger  for many American  and now that Donald Trump is president  and Republicans control both houses of  Congress the concern about these voter  suppression efforts is only growing  Trump's election integrity commission  met for the first time on July 19th and  the Commission had previously requested  voting data from every state the kind of  data that the Commission requested is  the same kind of data North Carolina  lawmakers used to craft their racist  voter suppression legislation  considering all of the fear-mongering  about voter fraud coming from Trump and  other top Republicans many experts are  concerned that the Commission will use  the data to recommend similar measures  on the national level a number of states  initially refused the Commission's  request including Pennsylvania whose  Governor Tom wolf wrote a blistering  letter to Co Bach in response federal  courts can still for states like  Pennsylvania to comply with the  Commission's request any further voting  restrictions that could come out of  Trump's crusade could really be  devastating the u.s. already has an  abysmal voter turnout rate and American  democracy is already plagued by many  shortcomings far more serious than voter  fraud it sometimes feels like some  Americans think that the u.s. is the  only democratic country in the world and  we regularly pat ourselves on the back  for being such a free and fair society  we like to think that America is the  shining city on a hill that we have the  best democracy in the world but the  truth is the electoral integrity project  a nonprofit that monitors elections  around the world that is widely  considered the most reputable of such  organizations ranked the u.s. 50 second  out of 153 countries in the 2016  perceptions of electoral integrity index  quote displaying the worst performance  among equivalent Western democracies the  EPI cited some of the same voter  suppression tactics we've already gone  over as well as the practice of  gerrymandering as reasons for America's  poor rankings moreover the man who  created the criteria that the EPI uses  for grading elections Andrew Reynolds an  expert of international electoral  integrity and political science  professor at the University of North  Carolina found that North Carolina would  not even be classified as fully  functioning democracy if it were its own  country  the problem with American democracy is  not that we have too many people who are  voting who aren't supposed to it's that  we don't have enough participation in  our elections particularly from the  segments of society that the Republicans  are tactically trying to keep out of the  process just fifty five point four  percent of all eligible voters cast  ballots in 2016  compare that to the turnout and the  recent French elections where nearly  sixty eight percent of eligible citizens  voted and that was the worst turnout in  modern history the top countries in the  world like Belgium and Sweden have voter  turnouts  in the high 80 percents so we should be  embarrassed by the lack of democratic  participation in this country and we  should be doing everything we can to  make it easier for people to vote not  harder  the real threat to the integrity of  American democracy  has nothing to do with so-called illegal  aliens or dead people voting and it has  everything to do with conservatives  cowardly and in unamerican ways trying  to disenfranchise the most vulnerable  among us

