publicans have used for decades  to argue for voting restrictions such as  voter ID laws that ultimately serve to  disenfranchise significant portions of  vulnerable demographics that have been  shown to disproportionately vote for  Democrats the Brookings Institution  argued in a 2016 report that these voter  ID laws systematically disenfranchised  minorities young people and the poor  adding that they are only one of several  practices that add up to a widespread  effort at election discrimination  others include purges of the voting  rolls reductions in early voting  opportunities and the closing of polling  stations voter ID laws currently exist  in 20 states and the practices of  purging voter rolls in  early voting and the number of polling  stations is widespread throughout the  country in 2016 under the guise of  improving election integrity the  Republican dominated North Carolina  legislature enacted a voter ID law that  also eliminated same-day voter  registration and eliminated seven days  of early voting as well as putting an  end to out of precinct voting it was  struck down by a federal court which  found that the law had discriminatory  intent and that it targeted  african-americans with almost surgical  precision thousands of pages of  documents were released as part of the  case and some showed Republican  lawmakers specifically asking for data  sorted by race the judges noted that the  data that the North Carolina lawmakers  requested before crafting the law showed  that African Americans  disproportionately lacked the most  common kind of photo ID those issued by  the Department of Motor Vehicles the  data also showed that black voters were  more likely to make use of early voting  and that counties with Sunday voting in  2014 were disproportionately black and  Democratic after reviewing that data the  legislature passed the bill eliminating  early voting the judges wrote that thus  in what comes as close to a smoking gun  as we are likely to see in modern times  the states very justification for a  challenged statute hinges explicitly on  race specifically it's concerned that  African Americans who had overwhelmingly  voted for Democrats had too much access  to the franchise while governor pat  mccrory insisted that the North Carolina  laws only purpose was to combat voter  fraud longtime Republican consultant  Carter Wren who the Washington Post  described as a fixture in North Carolina  politics admitted that of course it's  political why else would you do it  look if African Americans voted  overwhelmingly Republican they would  have kept early voting right where it  was  Renne said it wasn't about  discriminating against African Americans  they just ended up in the middle of it  because they vote for Democrats these  racist voter restriction laws are a  completely insidious cowardly pathetic  shameful attempt by Republicans to  suppress the vote of vulnerable  demographics who won't vote for them  instead of going out and trying to  earn people's votes Republicans are  trying to shut them out of the ballot  box and robbed them of their right to  vote in the context of our country's  long and ugly history of barring people  of color from the ballot box the  discriminatory aspect of these voter  suppression laws is outright shameful  but even still the targeting of certain  demographics has not been exclusively  reserved for minorities students and  poor whites have also become the targets  of the Republican crusade to suppress  the vote in leaked emails Green Bay City  Clerk  Chris teske an appointee of Republican  mayor Jim Schmidt admitted that the city  was trying to hinder college students  from voting because they tend to vote  Democratic leaders of eight different  student groups at the University of  wisconsin-green Bay had asked the city  to put an early voting location on  campus to alleviate long lines which  prevented many students from voting in  the 2016 primaries the request was  denied and in an email to a colleague on  the matter Teske wrote I feel by asking  for this to be the site for early voting  is encouraging the students to vote more  than benefiting the city as a whole I  have heard it said that students lean  more toward the Democrats a lawsuit  filed in Alabama in 2015 cites the  example of a woman from a rural area who  can't vote because she lacks a driver's  license according to the lawsuit she  needs to get a state issued voter ID at  the DMV but the one nearest her is open  one day per month there's no public  transportation to another DMV 40 miles  away roundtrip with more opening hours  according to a study from NYU's Brennan  Center 11% of voting-age citizens  nationally lack necessary photo ID while  many people in rural areas have trouble  accessing ID offices just think of the  burden that these restrictions put on  people who lack the time and resources  necessary to acquire proper ID or the  countless people who were discouraged  from voting because of needlessly long  lines caused by local polling stations  being closed while voter fraud is a  phantom issue intentional voter  suppression at the hands of Republican  state officials is a very real danger  for many American  and now that Donald Trump is president  and Republicans control both houses of  Congress the concern about these voter  suppression efforts is only growing  Trump's election integrity commission  met for the first time on July 19th and  the Commission had previously requested  voting data from every state the kind of  data that the Commission requested is  the same kind of data North Carolina  lawmakers used to craft their racist  voter suppression legislation  considering all of the fear-mongering  about voter fraud coming from Trump and  other top Republicans many experts are  concerned that the Commission will use  the data to recommend similar measures  on the national level a number of states  initially refused the Commission's  request including Pennsylvania whose  Governor Tom wolf wrote a blistering  letter to Co Bach in response federal  courts can still for states like  Pennsylvania to comply with the  Commission's request any further voting  restrictions that could come out of  Trump's crusade could really be  devastating the u.s. already has an  abysmal voter turnout rate and American  democracy is already plagued by many  shortcomings far more serious than voter  fraud it sometimes feels like some  Americans think that the u.s. is the  only democratic country in the world and  we regularly pat ourselves on the back  for being such a free and fair society  we like to think that America is the  shining city on a hill that we have the  best democracy in the world but the  truth is the electoral integrity project  a nonprofit that monitors elections  around the world that is widely  considered the most reputable of such  organizations ranked the u.s. 50 second  out of 153 countries in the 2016  perceptions of electoral integrity index  quote displaying the worst performance  among equivalent Western democracies the  EPI cited some of the same voter  suppression tactics we've already gone  over as well as the practice of  gerrymandering as reasons for America's  poor rankings moreover the man who  created the criteria that the EPI uses  for grading elections Andrew Reynolds an  expert of international electoral  integrity and political science  professor at the University of North  Carolina found that North Carolina would  not even be classified as fully  functioning democracy if it were its own  country  the problem with American democracy is  not that we have too many people who are  voting who aren't supposed to it's that  we don't have enough participation in  our elections particularly from the  segments of society that the Republicans  are tactically trying to keep out of the  process just fifty five point four  percent of all eligible voters cast  ballots in 2016  compare that to the turnout and the  recent French elections where nearly  sixty eight percent of eligible citizens  voted and that was the worst turnout in  modern history the top countries in the  world like Belgium and Sweden have voter  turnouts  in the high 80 percents so we should be  embarrassed by the lack of democratic  participation in this country and we  should be doing everything we can to  make it easier for people to vote not  harder  the real threat to the integrity of  American democracy  has nothing to do with so-called illegal  aliens or dead people voting and it has  everything to do with conservatives  cowardly and in unamerican ways trying  to disenfranchise the most vulnerable  among us
 

